  "summary": "Claire Lomas, a 44-year-old charity campaigner from Melton, Leicestershire, has died following an accident in Jordan on 22 August. Her family expressed devastation and asked for privacy as they grieve. Lomas was paralysed from the chest down after a riding accident at the Osberton Horse Trials in 2007, where she was thrown from her horse and suffered a broken neck, back, ribs, and a punctured lung. Despite her injuries, she raised nearly £1 million for charity through various athletic events. She gained widespread attention in 2012 when she completed the London Marathon in 17 days using a bionic suit, and was later chosen to light the Paralympic cauldron in Trafalgar Square in front of then-Prime Minister David Cameron and then-Mayor of London Boris Johnson. In 2016, she completed the Great North Run in five days while pregnant and wearing her bionic suit, after being denied a place in the Vitality London 10k."
